</td>\n    <td class=\"tg-cyim\"></td>\n    <td class=\"tg-pjk9\"></td>\n    <td class=\"tg-pjk9\">t<sub>BuOH</sub></td>\n  </tr>\n  <tr>\n    <td class=\"tg-pjk9\">CH<sub>3</sub>CHO</td>\n    <td class=\"tg-cyim\"></td>\n    <td class=\"tg-pjk9\"></td>\n    <td class=\"tg-pjk9\">MeOH</td>\n  </tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<br>The best combination is ","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"CH<sub>3</sub>CHO and MeOH"},{"identifier":"B","content":"HCHO and MeOH"},{"identifier":"C","content":"CH<sub>3</sub>CHO and tBuOH"},{"identifier":"D","content":"HCHO and tBuOH "}],"correct_options":["B"],"answer":null,"explanation":"In Formaldehyde (HCHO) both side of the C atom H presents but in Acetaldehyde (CH<sub>3</sub>CHO) one side of C atom -CH<sub>3</sub> group and other side H atom presents. So Formaldehyde is less crowded then it is more reactive.\n<br><br>CH<sub>3</sub>OH is 1<sup>o</sup> alcohol and t<sub>BuOH</sub> is 3<sup>o</sup> alcohol. As we know on 3<sup>o</sup> alcohol steric hindrance is very high compare to 1<sup>o</sup> alcohol and due to steric hindrance reactivity of 3<sup>o</sup> alcohol is less compare to 1<sup>o</sup> alcohol."},"hi":{"content":"निम्नलिखित प्रतिक्रिया में : \n<br><br> एल्डिहाइड + एल्कोहल   <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"3.706ex\" height=\"2.988ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -1309.5 1638 1320.5\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"REL\"><g data-mml-node=\"mover\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"27F6\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-27F6\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(131,711) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D43B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D43B\"></u